1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,712
An AI drafts the reply.

2
00:00:02,712 --> 00:00:04,561
Then it stops.

3
00:00:04,561 --> 00:00:07,087
You decide whether it gets sent.

4
00:00:07,783 --> 00:00:09,824
That pause matters.

5
00:00:09,824 --> 00:00:13,255
Meta's Muse can work across connected apps.

6
00:00:13,255 --> 00:00:15,959
But what are you actually handing over?

7
00:00:16,617 --> 00:00:21,311
On September twenty ninth, Meta announced Muse for Small Business.

8
00:00:21,311 --> 00:00:26,170
This week's story is about doing the work, not just answering questions.

9
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:29,082
Picture a small shop.

10
00:00:29,082 --> 00:00:31,876
Customer questions in one place.

11
00:00:31,876 --> 00:00:33,789
Sales in another.

12
00:00:33,789 --> 00:00:36,557
Tomorrow's content still waiting.

13
00:00:36,557 --> 00:00:39,669
The owner is the connection between everything.

14
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:43,143
A chatbot might suggest a reply.

15
00:00:43,143 --> 00:00:48,475
You copy it, switch apps, check the order, and finish the job yourself.

16
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:52,683
An agent can take steps through connected tools.

17
00:00:52,683 --> 00:01:00,183
Meta lists integrations including Shopify, Canva, Slack, and business accounts on Instagram and Facebook.

18
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:05,199
Here is an illustrative workflow, not a recording of Muse.

19
00:01:05,199 --> 00:01:07,853
A customer asks about delivery.

20
00:01:07,853 --> 00:01:11,220
The task is to prepare a useful response.

21
00:01:11,917 --> 00:01:18,651
The important part is context: which order, which delivery date, and what the shop has actually promised.

22
00:01:18,651 --> 00:01:21,355
A smooth sentence is not enough.

23
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:27,394
Meta says Muse can flag emails needing a response and write first drafts.

24
00:01:27,394 --> 00:01:32,152
That could move the owner from starting every task to reviewing prepared work.

25
00:01:32,817 --> 00:01:35,572
But preparation is not permission.

26
00:01:35,572 --> 00:01:40,495
Meta says publishing, sending, and spending require your approval.

27
00:01:40,495 --> 00:01:43,442
The send button is still your decision.

28
00:01:44,117 --> 00:01:48,721
Imagine the draft promises Friday, while the order says Monday.

29
00:01:48,721 --> 00:01:53,619
Approving that message quickly would turn a small error into a customer problem.

30
00:01:54,283 --> 00:01:58,352
So the useful test is not whether the reply sounds human.

31
00:01:58,352 --> 00:02:03,123
It is whether the facts, recipient, and proposed action are right.

32
00:02:03,817 --> 00:02:09,467
Meta says people choose connected apps and their access, and can disconnect a service.

33
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:13,255
Start with the narrowest access that lets the task work.

34
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:17,203
Muse runs on a separate cloud computer.

35
00:02:17,203 --> 00:02:21,514
Meta describes a separate Sentinel checking outgoing actions.

36
00:02:21,514 --> 00:02:26,157
These are company safeguards, not proof that every task is safe.

37
00:02:26,817 --> 00:02:33,857
Try one low-stakes job first: prepare a draft, compare it with the original, and count the corrections.

38
00:02:33,857 --> 00:02:37,582
Include checking time when judging any time saved.

39
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:43,531
The business announcement describes Muse as available in the United States and Canada.

40
00:02:43,531 --> 00:02:47,931
Check your own account's connectors and access before planning around it.

41
00:02:48,617 --> 00:02:52,086
The opportunity is less switching between apps.

42
00:02:52,086 --> 00:02:55,428
The responsibility is more careful review.

43
00:02:55,428 --> 00:02:58,999
Would you trust it to draft, send, or spend?

44
00:02:58,999 --> 00:03:01,436
Tell us where your line is.

45
00:03:02,117 --> 00:03:08,545
For a practical way to judge the next update, watch our video on what makes AI advice age badly.

46
00:03:08,545 --> 00:03:13,277
Subscribe for clear visual stories about what changed and why it matters.
